This not only confirms that at least some dinosaurs had feathers, a theory already widely accepted, but also provides details that fossils typically cannot offer, such as coloration and three-dimensional structure.
Scientists say the animal's feather (plume) had a yellowish tint on the upper side and was orangish-brown on the lower side due to carotenoid pigmentation.
This research also provides insight into the evolution of wings in birds.
Since the dinosaur's wing lacked a well-developed shaft, known as the rachis, it still possessed other wing features—distal shaft and tip—leading researchers to conclude that the later evolutionary development of additional features in the rachis was possible.
The structure of the feathers suggests they were not used for flight, but rather for regulating or dissipating body temperature. Researchers proposed that although this is a young dinosaur's tail, the specimen displays adult feathers and does not show the fine internal feathers typical of a modern bird.
