In France, the voting process has traditionally been a low-tech experience: voters isolate themselves in voting booths, mark their preferred candidate on a pre-printed paper ballot, and then place it into an envelope.
After the official verifies the voter's identity, the voter places the envelope into the ballot box and signs the voting register.
French election law strictly regulates these actions.
Since 1988, ballot boxes must be transparent to enable voters and observers to verify that no ballot envelopes were present at the start of voting and no envelopes were added except those from authorized voters who have been properly counted.
Candidates have the right to send their representatives to oversee every aspect of the process. In the evening, the poll workers conduct the vote counting under strict supervision and according to specific regulations.
