The cheetah is part of the group (genus Panthera) that includes lions, leopards, and jaguars. These four are the cats that can roar.
A cheetah's roar is not as powerful and deep as a lion's roar; rather, it is more similar to sharp, irritated cries.
The cheetah prefers to eat small animals. It hunts monkeys, snakes, rats, and if possible, birds too. The animals hunted by the cheetah are considerably smaller than itself.
Scientists believe that wild cheetahs track their prey by scent and follow them by sniffing the area where they live, and also search for animals.
They can see well and move forward stealthily in the darkness through their night vision. The savannah cheetah blends with its surroundings and launches attacks on its prey, catching them effectively.
When a small group of organisms (a small population) becomes separated from a larger population (for example, if they cross a mountain range or a river, or if they move to a new island, from where they can no longer return easily), they are often found in a different environment than before.
In the new environment, there are diverse types of resources and various competitors. Therefore, there will be a greater need for different features or upgrades in products, compared to what was previously required.
There has been no change at all in the original population, and they still require the same level of compatibility as before.
As time passes and the new community adapts to its new environment, they gradually begin to resemble the other communities.
Eventually, after thousands or perhaps hundreds of thousands of years, these two populations will become so different in appearance that they can no longer be classified as the same species.
We refer to this process as speciation, which essentially means the formation of new species. Speciation is an inevitable outcome and a very important part of evolution.
