Taiwan's history dates back to the 15th century, when passing European navigators recorded the island's name as "Ilha Formosa," meaning "Beautiful Island."
In 1624, the Dutch East India Company established a base in southwestern Taiwan, initiated changes in local grain production methods, and employed Chinese laborers to work on their rice fields and plantations.
In 1683, the armies of the Qing Dynasty (1644–1912) seized control of the western and eastern coastal regions of Taiwan, and in 1885, Taiwan was established as a province of the Qing Dynasty.
In 1895, after its defeat in the First Sino-Japanese War (1894–1895), the Qing government signed the Treaty of Shimonoseki, under which it recognized Japan's right to take over its authority over Taiwan, leading to Japanese rule over the island until 1945.
