Following the early Middle Ages, the High Middle Ages were succeeded by the Late Middle Ages, which ended socially around 1500.
The theory of technological determinism is a term that encompasses a wide range of ideas, from the notion of technological progress or the inevitability of modern technology, to the strong belief that human destiny is governed by an underlying logic rooted in scientific laws and the manifestation of technology.
Most interpretations of technological determinism share two common notions: first, that technological development follows a path that transcends cultural or political influences, and second, that technology exerts effects on societies that are not socially conditioned but rather inherited or predetermined.
For example, one could say that a motorcar necessarily smoothens the way for road development.
However, it is not economically viable to lay down a road network across the country for just a few vehicles, so new production methods have been introduced to reduce the cost of car ownership.
The widespread ownership of cars also leads to a high rate of accidents on roads, prompting the development of new technologies in the healthcare sector to enable the repair of damaged bodies.
In Romanticism, there is a significant element of cultural determination, drawn from writers such as Goethe, Gottlieb Fichte, and Schiller.
Within the context of Romanticism, geography shaped individuals, and over time, the traditions and cultures associated with that geography remained in harmony with societal needs, proving to be more effective and meaningful than rigid, formal laws.
Just as Paris is regarded today as a center of fashion, Constantinople was considered the center of fashion in medieval Europe.
It is known to have begun around 400 CE as a center of luxury and prosperity, and it remained in existence until the 11th century CE.
The main reason for its decline during the 12th century was that the Crusaders returned home carrying silk and spices as gifts, which were worth more in Byzantine markets than the prices offered.
This was the time when the title of fashion capital shifted from Constantinople to Paris.
Gothic style flourished between the 10th and 11th centuries and lasted until the 14th century.
Clothing was initially greatly influenced by Byzantine culture in the East.
However, due to slow communication infrastructure, the West could fall behind by 25 to 30 years.
In the later period of the Middle Ages, Western Europe began advancing its fashion style. One of the most significant developments at that time was the widespread adoption of buttons to fasten clothing, particularly among the Crusaders.
Subsistence farming is a type of farming that is carried out solely to provide food up to the extent of meeting the needs of the farmer and his family.
Sustainable farming is often simple and organic, achieving maximum yield by combining crop rotation or other relatively easy techniques with the use of locally adapted, environmentally safe seeds, thus maintaining the ecological balance.
Historically, most farmers were engaged in agriculture for their livelihood, and this situation still persists in many developing countries.
Subcultures bring together like-minded individuals who feel overlooked by societal standards and enable them to develop a sense of identity.
Subcultures can be specific due to the members' age, ethnicity, social class, location, and/or gender.
The characteristics that distinguish a subculture may include linguistic, aesthetic, religious, political, sexual, geographical, or other factors.
Members of subcultures often signal their membership through distinctive and symbolic styles, bodily fashion, specific mannerisms, and unique ways of speaking.
One of the most common ways used to highlight social significance is identifying, during their upbringing, a few unpleasant incidents in which children were deliberately or accidentally ignored by adults, subjected to neglect or mistreatment, and prevented from participating in social activities.
Such children are called "feral" or wild. In some cases, people (usually their parents) confine feral children; in other cases, the reason for distancing themselves from the child is that the parents deny the child's serious mental or physical disability.
The wild children must have endured severe mistreatment or trauma before being left behind or fleeing.
Others are believed to have been raised by animals; some are said to have been living in the forest in their own dens.
When raised entirely by non-human animals, wild children (within psychological limits) exhibit behavior nearly identical to that of the animals raising them, such as showing fear of humans or indifference toward them.
While project-based learning should be made easier and more interesting to learn, guided instruction provides an additional advantage.
It is not just a method of learning folding, but rather it supports individuals going through a new learning experience, such as using a new computer program or starting a new project.
Its folds can be both virtual and physical. In other words, a teacher is one kind of fold, just as the small paper figure in Microsoft Office is also a fold.
Virtual scaffolds are embedded internally within the software. Their purpose is to prompt, prepare, and explain strategies that may challenge the learner to solve problems independently.
Children are placed in foster care for various reasons, including neglect, abuse, and even extortion.
