Cross-ties were initially used to keep the tracks in place. But gradually, it was found that tracks would become more effective if iron plates were added on top of them.
This practice became common, but due to iron, wooden cart wheels began to feature greater embellishment.
Finally, the wooden wheels were replaced by iron wheels. The first fully iron rails were introduced in 1767.
