The Sundarbans is the world's largest coastal mangrove belt, stretching 80 kilometers (50 miles) from the coastal areas into the remote, underdeveloped regions of Bangladesh and India.
The Sundarbans have been designated as a UNESCO World Heritage site. The forest area located in the Indian part is known as Sundarbans National Park.
Forests are not just mangrove swamps — they also include the last remaining patches of vast forests that once covered the Gangetic plains.
The Sundarbans cover an area of 3,850 km², of which one-third consists of water/swampy regions.
